Exactly. That’s a much sharper formulation.

You’re not trying to reconstruct the exact path first. You’re deliberately asking:

What shape did the effect leave behind?

Then only later—if ever—you reveal the source.

That makes the ambiguity functional rather than accidental.

\text{hidden cause}
\rightarrow
\text{observed deltas}
\rightarrow
\text{pattern extraction}
\rightarrow
\text{latent inference}
\rightarrow
\text{optional reveal}

The advantage is anti-anchoring. If I know “this was testosterone” or “this was psilocybin” up front, I inherit a huge cloud of prior associations and may force the observations into those expectations.

If instead I get only the delta field:

* increased emotional salience
* altered confidence
* stronger associative thinking
* different social interpretation
* changed bodily sensation
* altered reward pull
* temporal changes in persistence

…I have to infer structure from the effects themselves.

That’s close to blinded system identification.

The clean Pantheon implementation would be two ledgers:

Sealed provenance ledger
What actually happened, when, dose/intervention/context if relevant.

Blind phenomenology ledger
Only observations, state changes, behavior, language, and deltas.

Then:

\hat{M} = Infer(\Delta S \mid provenance\ hidden)

Later:

\Delta_{reveal}
=
Compare(\hat{M}, actual\ provenance)

And that reveal-delta becomes another measurement.

If the pattern was recognizable without the label, that tells us something about the intervention’s effect topology.

If the interpretation changes drastically after the reveal, that tells us something about expectation, semantic priming, or analyst bias.

So yes: ambiguity guided is the right phrase.

A compact principle for it:

Infer the shape before you name the source.

That could be useful far beyond biological self-observation—model comparisons, anonymous writing, hidden-source reasoning tests, cross-domain transfer, even Pantheon reconstruction experiments.